      "content": "That's a really nuanced way to frame it. To really ground this in the research, I'm looking for more specific \"indicators\" that a sociologist or an officer might actually observe. \n\nWhen you talk about those cultural dimensions and the \"inherent suspicion\" or specific \"defensive body language\" in these Black or Latino communities, what does that actually look like in a real-world encounter? For example, are there specific non-verbal cues—like certain ways of standing, specific types of vocal inflections, or even patterns of avoiding or seeking eye contact—that are more characteristic of these groups when they feel that tension? I'm trying to see if I can categorize these observable behaviors to differentiate them from general, non-ethnic-specific stress responses.",
